20:33.540 --> 20:38.280
You break the space down into smaller, manageable, overlapping patches or local neighborhoods.

20:38.500 --> 20:40.480
Okay. And the recursive part?

20:40.580 --> 20:44.540
Recursive means you do this hierarchically from the macro scale down to the micro scale.

20:44.860 --> 20:47.180
Okay. So we have overlapping tiles of data.

20:47.760 --> 20:49.980
Where does the trajectory awareness come in?

20:49.980 --> 20:57.380
This is where TARTAN deploys some incredibly heavy mathematics, specifically, sheaf theory and cohomology.

20:58.120 --> 21:04.220
I'm going to need a lifeline here. What is sheaf theory, mechanically speaking? Let's keep it out of the chalkboards if we can.

21:04.440 --> 21:04.920
Fair enough.

21:04.920 --> 21:11.400
At its core, sheaf theory is the rigorous mathematics of local to global compatibility.

21:12.020 --> 21:12.300
Okay.

21:12.520 --> 21:14.280
Let's go back to your overlapping tiles.

21:14.940 --> 21:20.040
Each individual tile might look perfectly rational and consistent when you examine it in isolation.

21:20.840 --> 21:23.680
A sheaf is the algebraic structure that checks the seams.

21:23.980 --> 21:25.240
The seams between the tiles.

21:25.240 --> 21:30.460
Exactly. It asks, where tile A overlaps with tile B, do their local truths agree?

21:31.080 --> 21:39.040
And more importantly, if I stitch 100 tiles together in a circle, does the total structure lay flat and mathematically coherent, or does it tear itself apart?

21:39.340 --> 21:45.180
So it's an admissibility auditor. It checks if local data can successfully be glued into a global reality.

21:45.320 --> 21:50.600
Exactly. It tests for the existence of what mathematicians call a global section.

21:50.600 --> 21:57.120
